{
  "description": "CommandFingerprint(command) identifies a management command's input, so a command id reused with different input is a conflict while the same input replays, whichever SDK sent it. It is the lowercase hex SHA-256 of the UTF-8 bytes of a canonical JSON object with these members, each left out when absent or at its default: \"kind\" (always); \"ids\" (the target ids in the order given, as lowercase hyphenated UUIDs; left out when empty); \"filter\" (the target filter) and \"max\" (left out when 0); \"dry_run\", \"cascade\", \"reset_attempts\" (left out when false); \"reason\", \"name\" (the signal name) and \"version\" (the repin target version) (each left out when empty); \"at\" (the reschedule time in UTC truncated to milliseconds, formatted YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.sssZ); \"priority\" (left out when 0); \"payload\" (the signal payload; left out when absent or null). A filter is an object with \"and\" and \"or\" (arrays of filters, left out when empty), \"not\" (a filter), \"field\" and \"op\" (left out when empty) and \"value\" (left out when null). Canonical JSON has no whitespace; object members are sorted by key in code point order; strings escape only the quotation mark, the reverse solidus and U+0000 to U+001F (as \\b, \\t, \\n, \\f, \\r, else \\u00xx in lowercase hex), every other character literal; numbers are written as ECMAScript's Number.prototype.toString writes them (integers without a fraction; exponent form, as in 1e+21 and 1e-7, at or above 1e21 and below 1e-6). The command id itself is not part of the input.",
